WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Hey, everyone. Patrick here in Vancouver. Welcome to Planet Terri and daily episode 45.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.440
It's been another week where the boundaries between different areas of medicine feel like

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.680
they're blurring in really interesting ways. We're seeing cell therapies originally built for

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.720
cancer now, rewriting the rules for autoimmune disease, decades old biological insights, finally

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:25.040
turning into drugable targets, and a steady flow of capital into both clever delivery technologies

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.080
and more traditional devices and drugs. Let's walk through what stood out.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.480
The story that really stopped me in my tracks is the first reported case of a single

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.040
CAR-T infusion driving three distinct autoimmune diseases into remission in the same patient.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:44.220
Researchers treated an individual who was simultaneously dealing with severe systemic

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:51.400
lupus erythematosis, chogren syndrome, and myasthenia gravis. After one dose of autologous CAR-T

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.940
cells directed against CD19, all three conditions went into deep remission.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.000
What's striking is how complete the reset appears to have been. The therapy didn't simply suppress

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.620
symptoms. It eliminated the rogue B cell clones that were driving the autoimmunity and,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:12.980
in the process, seemed to wipe out the aberrant immune memory that had kept those diseases active

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.780
for years. This is a fundamentally different approach from the way we've historically managed

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:24.340
overlapping autoimmune syndromes. Instead of layering immunosuppressants or biologics that each target

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:29.620
one pathway, the CAR-T therapy appears to go upstream and reset the immune system at its source.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.800
Of course, we're talking about a single patient, so we have to be measured. Still,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.360
the depth and breadth of the response suggests that properly engineered cell therapies

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.980
might be able to address the shared underlying B cell dysfunction that links many autoimmune

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:50.000
conditions. I keep thinking about the patients I've known who live with multiple overlapping diagnoses,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.120
the complexity of their care is enormous. If this kind of multi-disease remission holds up in larger

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:01.460
trials, it could dramatically change how we think about eligibility and sequencing of advanced therapies.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.820
Clinicians are already planning longer follow-up to understand how durable these remissions prove to be

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.940
and whether patients eventually need retreatment. The fact that a modality born in oncology is now

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.440
showing this kind of power and immunology feels like one of those quiet but profound shifts.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:21.480
The first regulatory approvals for autoimmune CAR-T are starting to look like they're on the horizon,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.220
and this case will only accelerate that conversation. While that one remarkable immune reset has

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:33.020
everyone talking about cellular medicine, the biotech world is also pouring serious energy into an

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.340
entirely different, much older molecular target that has suddenly become one of the hottest areas

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:46.560
in cardiovascular drug development, the molecule is lipoprotein A, or LPA. It was first identified

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.340
back in the early 1960s by the Norwegian physician Korra Berg while he was searching for new blood

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.380
group antigens. At the time, nobody could have predicted it would become a central focus of

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:05.140
cardiometabolic research 60 years later. LPA is almost entirely genetically determined. Unlike LDL

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:11.220
cholesterol, it barely budges in response to diet, exercise, weight loss, or even statin therapy.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.260
That's been frustrating for both patients and clinicians for decades. Levels above 50 milligrams per

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.960
desoleter, which according to population data, affect roughly one in five adults worldwide,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.440
are associated with a substantially elevated lifetime risk of heart attack, ischemic stroke,

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:35.040
and aortic valve stenosis. What makes the particle particularly nasty is its dual payload. It carries

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:41.680
oxidized phospholipids and cholesterol, but it also contains a polypoprotein A, a protein that promotes

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.500
clotting and drives inflammation. In essence, it delivers a double-hit to artery walls. It accelerates

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:53.380
plaque formation while making the plaques more likely to rupture or trigger thrombosis. For a long time,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:00.220
LPA was treated as an interesting but unmodifiable risk factor. You measured it once, noted it in the chart

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.520
and moved on because there wasn't much you could do. That picture is changing rapidly.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.180
Several RNA-based and gene-silencing drugs are now in late-stage clinical trials, specifically designed

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:18.180
to lower LPA production at the genetic level in the liver. For the first time, we may soon have therapies

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:24.040
that can actually address this inherited risk directly. The practical takeaway feels straightforward.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:29.360
If you have a family history of early heart disease, despite seemingly well-controlled LDL,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:35.000
or if you've had cardiovascular events at a younger age than expected, it's worth asking your doctor

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.640
for a one-time LPA test. Knowing your number today could become clinically actionable in the next

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:48.960
few years. The surge of interest in LPA is a classic case of how a long understood biological insight

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:55.120
can sit on the shelf until the right therapeutic technology finally catches up. And that same

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.380
pattern of scientific readiness creating commercial opportunity is showing up across several deals

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:07.900
this week. One of the clearest examples is California-based MedTech Company E2, which just closed

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:12.740
an $80 million series C to commercialize its next generation thrombectomy device.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:18.100
The technology is aimed at patients with life-threatening pulmonary embolisms, where rapid and

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.780
complete clot removal can be the difference between recovery and serious long-term complications.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:28.260
The fact that investors are writing big checks for improved mechanical solutions in acute

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:34.040
cardiovascular care tells you that the unmet need remains high, even as pharmacological options expand.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.700
It's a reminder that not every advance has to be a molecular one. Sometimes better hardware

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.860
still moves the needle. Money is clearly flowing into both novel drugs and devices right now,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.760
but some of the most intriguing activity is happening in the often overlooked science of delivery,

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:55.620
particularly for genetic medicines. Estella's pharma exercised a $15 million option

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:02.160
to license especially engineered adino-associated virus capsid developed by dynotherapeutics.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:07.200
The capsid was designed for improved delivery to skeletal muscle, a tissue that has historically

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.820
been difficult to target efficiently with gene therapies. This deal builds on a research collaboration

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:18.940
that began in 2021. The precision now possible in viral vector engineering is impressive.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:25.480
By tailoring the outer shell of the virus, scientists can improve tropism, reduce off-target

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:30.680
distribution to the liver, and potentially lower the overall dose needed. That matters for both

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:36.700
safety and cost. Muscle disorders such as certain muscular distrophies have been waiting for therapies

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:41.620
that can actually reach enough of the affected tissue. Deals like this one suggests the field is

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:46.600
getting closer to making those therapies practical at scale. Delivery has always been the quiet

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.740
bottleneck in gene therapy, watching it gradually loosen feels like genuine progress.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:57.400
Improving how we get potent molecules to the right cells is also a central challenge in the

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:02.560
antibody drug conjugate field which continues to be one of the most active areas in oncology.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:09.620
Akari Therapeutics announced a partnership with Wushi-XDC to develop and manufacture its ADC candidate.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:15.220
The collaboration is structured to accelerate both clinical testing and crucially future commercial

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:20.380
scale production. These specialized manufacturing partnerships have become essential because the

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:25.880
chemistry of attaching a powerful toxin to an antibody while keeping the whole construct stable

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:31.820
is extraordinarily complex. The fact that companies are investing early in manufacturing scale-up

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:37.600
signals confidence that their programs will need to move quickly if early data continue to look promising.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:44.900
That momentum is even more visible at GSK. The company's head of oncology R&amp;D recently outlined plans for

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:50.100
a whole series of phase three studies involving its Hanso partnered antibody drug conjugate.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:55.780
He spoke about the need for scientific courage as the company pushes forward following encouraging

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:00.900
data readouts. It's always interesting to hear senior leaders talk about courage in the context of

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:06.260
large late stage trials. It reminds you how much conviction is required to invest hundreds of millions

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:11.900
of dollars and years of patient effort on the strength of earlier signals. GSK's aggressive posture

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:17.360
here is part of a broader industry trend. Once an ADC platform shows it can deliver meaningful efficacy

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:22.520
with a manageable safety profile, companies are willing to bet big on expanding into multiple tumor

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:28.720
types and earlier lines of therapy. Of course, not every program advances smoothly. The FDA has

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.580
issued a second complete response letter to Replomune for its on-calytic immunotherapy RP-1 in advanced

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:40.100
melanoma. The initial rejection came last July and despite the company's efforts to address the

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:45.920
agency's concerns, regulators were not satisfied. This is a tough setback for a novel approach that

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.960
uses a modified herpes virus to both kill tumor cells directly and stimulate a broader immune response.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:57.760
The company now has to decide whether to generate more data, modify the product, or potentially pivot.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:03.500
These moments are painful but not uncommon. They're part of the long uneven path that new modalities

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:08.480
have to walk before they become standard of care. While some developers hit regulatory walls,

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:14.480
others are raising substantial capital to keep pushing forward. Chinese KRT company or a cell

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:21.060
therapeutics just closed its final private funding round, bringing in more than $110 million as it

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:26.700
prepares for an eventual public listing. The money will support its pipeline of cell therapies

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:31.920
focused on solid tumors, particularly carcinomas. It's another sign that investors continue to believe

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.180
the biggest remaining opportunity in cell therapy lies beyond hematologic cancers,

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:42.100
getting KRT cells to work reliably in solid tumors remains enormously challenging because of the

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:47.860
immunosuppressive microenvironment, antigen heterogeneity, and trafficking issues. Companies that

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:53.940
can make even incremental progress on those fronts are attracting serious attention. CART is far

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:59.560
from the only modality seeing fresh capital. Avalon pharma is preparing for an initial public offering

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:04.800
to fund phase three trials of its inhaled formulations of medicines that are already approved for

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:10.660
respiratory diseases. The strategy is straightforward but smart. Take drugs with well-understood

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:15.280
safety profiles and deliver them directly to the lungs at higher local concentrations while

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.840
reducing systemic side effects. For patients with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis or other serious

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.820
interstitial lung diseases, this could meaningfully improve both efficacy and tolerability.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:31.260
The continued interest in reformulating existing therapies for better organ specific delivery

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:35.520
shows how much room there still is to optimize medicines we've been using for years.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:41.520
Not every partnership is headed toward an IPO, some are being restructured quietly to better align

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:48.360
incentives. Boringert Ingelheim is transferring marketing rights for the digital therapeutic CT-155,

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:53.300
a software-based treatment for schizophrenia that it had been developing with click therapeutics.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.960
Boringert is also providing additional funding as part of the revised agreement.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.520
This gives click greater control over commercialization of what could become one of the first

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:07.660
prescription digital therapeutics broadly used in psychiatry. The move reflects the growing

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:12.720
recognition that software-based interventions require different commercialization skills than

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:18.580
traditional pharmaceuticals. It will be fascinating to watch whether this new arrangement helps CT-155

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:24.380
reach more patients faster. While digital therapies are being repositioned, hardware and respiratory

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:30.600
care continues to face quality and safety challenges. Phillips has issued a voluntary recall of specific

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:36.100
software for its trilogy Evoventilators. The company is warning clinicians not to use certain

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:41.600
non-nomatic nebulizers in combination with the devices because of the risk that patients may receive

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.960
an underdose of their inhaled respiratory medication. These kinds of field actions are never ideal,

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:52.100
but they highlight the ongoing complexity of integrating multiple technologies at the bedside.

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:57.540
When patients are critically ill, even small deviations and drug or oxygen delivery can

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:03.560
matter, it's a sober reminder that in medicine, we are always balancing innovation with rigorous

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:10.100
attention to real-world performance. Finally this week, we see Big Pharma continuing to expand its

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:16.720
oncology toolkit through creative new mechanisms. Gilead Sciences paid Chimera Therapeutics $45

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:22.780
million up front to gain rights to a pre-clinical molecular glue degrader aimed at an undisclosed

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:29.540
cancer target. Molecular glues work by forcing two proteins together so that one tags the other for

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:34.780
destruction by the cell's natural disposal system. It's a fundamentally different approach from

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:40.000
traditional inhibitors and the space has seen intense activity in several high-profile acquisitions

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:45.600
recently. Gilead has been deliberately broadening its late-stage oncology portfolio and this deal

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:51.000
fits that pattern. Targeted protein degradation is still early, but the potential to go after proteins

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:57.060
once considered undruggable keeps drawing serious investment. Each new modality we add to the cancer

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.660
arsenal increases the chance that we can find the right combination for individual patients.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:06.500
That covers the main developments that caught my attention this week. Before we wrap up,

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:11.040
I'd suggest keeping an eye on two things in particular. First, watch how those single patient

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:16.040
CAR-T autoimmune results influence the design and patient selection for larger trials.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:20.980
The potential to treat multiple diseases with one intervention is too important to ignore.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:27.660
Second, consider asking for an LPA test at your next physical, especially of heart disease runs

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:33.040
in your family. As those silencing therapies move closer to approval, knowing your level could shift

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:37.640
from interesting trivia to clinically useful information. Thanks for spending time with me.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:42.300
If you found any of this useful, share the episode with someone who's curious about where medicine is

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:49.560
headed. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, see you next time on Planet Terri and Daily. 378, approximately 15.5

